From 2e242dadcafa74b7c237b0888a0803f8dfff0586 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: serlk <22211586+Serlk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 16:18:25 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 28/28] ipa: mtkcamraw: Exposure control from the processed
 picture

The ISP settings of the replayed requests are frozen: their statistics
format, colour gains and tone curve are not available, so the only
controls are the sensor's. The IPA runs libipa's mean luminance AGC on a
luma histogram of the output picture, which is on an encoded scale: the
histogram is linearised with a power law (encodingGamma, 2.2 by
default) first. The closed loop does not depend on that model being
exact. Exposure time comes first up to one frame at 30 fps, then
analogue gain; FrameDurationLimits can allow longer frames, for which
the vertical blanking follows the exposure. No digital gain can be
applied. The tuning file can ask for a minimum vertical blanking
(minVblank, 300 lines for the IMX766): the replayed ISP reports a frame
done ~0.5 ms after the last line, and with the shortest blanking that
falls on the next start of frame. The tuning file also gives the
sensor's exposure margin (48 lines), AE's own margin between exposure and
frame end (frameMargin, 256 lines: with 48 the replayed ISP lost frames),
the longest frame AE may use (maxFrameDuration, 66.7 ms: 15 fps in the
dark; the blanking follows the exposure) and AE's analogue gain limit
(16x of the IMX766's 64x), applied from the first frame.

The AGC computes only on a frame that shows the exposure it last asked
for (at most 4 frames of waiting): with the sensor's two-frame latency it
otherwise corrected the same error twice and swung at start-up. The
highlight constraint is an upper bound only: libipa's default lower bound
halves the exposure at most per step while the output is clipped.

Controls: AeEnable, ExposureTime, AnalogueGain (manual when AE is off),
ExposureValue, FrameDurationLimits, AeConstraintMode, AeExposureMode.
Metadata: ExposureTime, AnalogueGain, FrameDuration, AeEnable.

mtkcamraw_agc_test runs the module against a simulated camera (scene
radiances, sensor exposure x gain with clipping, power-law output) and
checks that a dark, a normal and a bright scene settle at the target
within 20 frames, with the sensor taking new controls two frames late
and left at 64x by an earlier session, the exposure at least frameMargin
lines inside the frame and the gain under the AE limit.

Signed-off-by: serlk <22211586+Serlk@users.noreply.github.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
